A||||11||DaveL||What's with these #!!*#% 2.5's||||5||
Z||000000||DaveL||07-12-2001||01:58 PM||||Well, my 87 SE 2.5 bit the dust, threw a rod or ate a valve or the like, and I don't have the money for a rebuilt or a rebuild, so I bought another 87 with a 2.5. It ran great but made a noise like a diesel at an idle. The timing gear went south 2 weeks after I got it, so i had that replaced. It still makes some similar noise at idle, but much less than it was. The noise disappears completely as soon as you hit the gas. Any educated guesses? I guess when I get the money, I'll take out the dead engine and rebuild it from bottom up, crank, camshaft, lifters, rods, pistons and rings, pushrods, head if needed...but untill then||||reg||5
Z||000001||Mach10||07-12-2001||04:13 PM||||Bad water pump? The other possibility is that your bottom end is shot, piston slap or connecting rod bearings worn. When the engine is under load (accelerating) the noise will quiet, and the rods push against the crank, but when free-wheeling (coasting or idle) they shimmy around, causing the noise...<BR>Sound right?||||reg||7
Z||000002||Brucepts||07-12-2001||06:30 PM||||Not to worry my 2.5 sounds the same, but with 156,000 miles on it I just turn up the radio put some 40wt oil in and the noise goes away. Just wait for the red lights on the dash to come on then you know you have a problem  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Disclaimer: All in jest do not take seriously  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> But I do use 40wt oil <P>------------------<BR>Advanced Dealer in the Great Material Continuum . . .<BR>Non Fiero Website: <A HREF="http://tractorsport.com" TARGET=_blank>http://tractorsport.com</A> <BR>You can't race a dyno . . .||||reg||1
Z||000003||DRH||07-12-2001||06:52 PM||||2.5s seem to be generally noisy motors. I have heard quite a few that sounded like diesels that seem to keep running just fine.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||07-12-2001||07:45 PM||||The valve train on the 87 DIS motor is inherantly noisy. I've not had the time to mess with why. I suspect the self limiting nuts are slightly off keeping the rockers a hair to high on the studs.<P>You can get these motors cheap from Grand Am and Olds Cutlass Calais. They were in the Olds from 87-91. I wouldn't waste a dime on a fried one.||||reg||1
Z||000005||My7Fieros||07-12-2001||09:31 PM||||Yup, I used to have an 89 Buick Skylark...same 2.5 engine...sounded like a diesel...just like everyone said, they're just loud engines. M7F||||reg||1
Z||000006||Hoostine||07-13-2001||12:11 AM||||"I don't have the money for a rebuilt or a rebuild, so I bought another 87 with a 2.5."<P>this is what i love about fiero's.  instead of buying a new motor, you save money buying a whole new car  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>--Hoostine||||reg||1
Z||000007||goldiron||07-13-2001||12:17 AM||||I had an odd knock/tap develop last year that only occured while was idleing in gear, but disappeared every time I gave it a little gas. <BR>After lots of investigation, mechanic put it on rack and finally chased it down to loose bolt on compressor bracket.||||reg||1
Z||000008||erikred||07-13-2001||10:12 AM||||2.5s do tend to be noisy. However, it sounds like you may have some bad wrist pins. My 88 ran very smooth and quiet until you hit the gas, then for a breif moment you could hear what sounded like a light rod slap or heavy ping. This only occured right when the engine reved. When I took the motor apart it was the piston pins that had too much clearance causing the pistons to slap around.||||reg||10
Z||000009||84Bill||07-14-2001||01:49 AM||||I've had 3 Duke equiped cars and every one of them had that desiel sound at idle and my 88 sounded like a freight train at cruise speed. that thing was gawd awfull compaired to my 84||||reg||14
Z||000010||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||07-14-2001||03:12 AM||||I have heard two Duke engines run without ticking.  <P>Maybe your problem is not inside the engine.<BR>Sealing a used head shield to the head (without a gasket as GM did) is not easy. This can cause a ticking noise! <P>Use a length of rubber tubing as a stethoscope while the engine makes the most noise (idle).  Listen around the EGR valve for a hissing/tapping noise. Then do the same around the exhaust manifold.  <P>If all this fails, then try plugging the exhaust with CLEAN rags while the car is running.  Leaks will sound like hissing.  You should wear gloves while doings this, dual exhuast tips mean two rags and two hands. <P>What is the intake manifold vacuum at idle?  <P>Vacuum gauges are not expensive ($10 or so).<p>[This message has been edited by LuckyTheyWereEverMade (edited 07-14-2001).]||||reg||12
Z||000011||FieroSoul||07-14-2001||07:58 AM||||  My 2.5 started making that diesel sound a while back. Several mechanics seemed to be stumped by it. One used a stethoscope and determined it was in the top end.  After doing a lifter job myself and several trips under the valve cover to check the rockers with no results, I gave up and tried one more shop. The guy found that the crank pulley bolt was loose and wobbling!  Chased the threads and lock-tite solved almost all of the problem!  <BR> Since you just went in after your timing gears, I wonder if your bolt has worked loose too?||||reg||6
